# YOLO26n end to end on the NPU: correct detections from the container
# Host DESKTOP-CBL5NUA (Desktop 2, Ryzen 7 8700G, XDNA1 Phoenix), 2026-09-20, env mlir-aie-iron
# Device 0; xrt-smi reported no hardware contexts before and after, and idle at the end.
#
## The decoder
#
# npu/yolo26_decode.py. YOLO26 dropped DFL, so its box branch regresses four values per anchor
# where YOLOv8 emits 4 x REG_MAX bins reduced through a softmax-weighted sum. Everything else
# is identical, which is why the result is a drop-in for npu.yolo.postprocess. Transcribed from
# the ops the export actually contains, read out of the graph rather than from the paper:
#
#   lt, rb = box[:, 0:2], box[:, 2:4]
#   x1y1   = anchors - lt ; x2y2 = anchors + rb
#   xywh   = concat([(x1y1 + x2y2) / 2, x2y2 - x1y1]) * strides
#   out    = concat([xywh, sigmoid(cls)])
#
# The anchor centres (0.5, 1.5, ...) and strides (8, 16, 32) were read from the graph's own
# constants and match npu.yolo_decode.anchors_and_strides.
#
## Checked against the export itself
#
#   decode_heads(cut float heads) vs the full float export's output0, bus.jpg:
#     box channels     max |diff| 0.000e+00      (bit-exact)
#     class channels   max |diff| 1.382e-07      (float32 sigmoid rounding)
#
## Detections on bus.jpg, conf 0.25, IoU 0.7, per-class NMS
#
#   full float export         5   c5@0.90, c0@0.85, c0@0.85, c0@0.84, c0@0.56
#   float cut + this decoder  5   c5@0.90, c0@0.85, c0@0.85, c0@0.84, c0@0.56   (identical)
#   XINT8 cut on ORT CPU      6   c5@0.92, c0@0.82, c0@0.82, c0@0.73, c0@0.73, c11@0.32
#   THE CONTAINER ON DEVICE 0 6   c5@0.924, c0@0.818, c0@0.818, c0@0.731, c0@0.731, c11@0.321
#
#     class  5 conf 0.924 box (7, 250, 790, 506)      bus
#     class  0 conf 0.818 box (61, 398, 176, 500)     person
#     class  0 conf 0.818 box (223, 412, 122, 439)    person
#     class  0 conf 0.731 box (0, 567, 61, 307)       person
#     class  0 conf 0.731 box (668, 392, 142, 472)    person
#     class 11 conf 0.321 box (2, 246, 34, 81)        stop sign
#
# The device reproduces the quantized CPU model exactly. Quantization COSTS nothing visible
# here and adds one low-confidence false positive (the stop sign at 0.32), which XINT8 also
# produces on CPU - it is the quantizer, not the engine.
#
## A trap worth recording
#
# The first device run gave 2 detections and looked like a decode bug. It was a stale
# container: build/yolo26n.ignite was compiled BEFORE the host-segment banding fix
# (results/aie/yolo26n_exact_20260920.log) and is wrong by construction. The fixed containers
# are yolo26n_band2.ignite and yolo26n_ctl.ignite. A container is a build artifact and goes
# stale the moment the compiler changes.
#
## NOT ESTABLISHED
#
#   - No mAP. Six detections on one image is a smoke test, not accuracy; the 500-image slice
#     rule applies doubly to a single frame.
#   - No latency result. The 14.089 ms above is one cold dispatch through a debug script that
#     reads six tensors back one at a time; it is not a G2G figure and not comparable to
#     anything.
#   - No comparison against AMD's stack.
#   - The decoder is numpy only. There is no native C path for it, where YOLOv8's decode has
#     one (decode_native.c), so a fair G2G comparison must either add one or say plainly that
#     the host tail is unoptimized.
